THE CHECK (check mode only; write mode is unchanged and never reads the template)
1. Required list. Read `<root>/.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-agent/assets/agent-template.md`.
   The list is the lines between `<!-- REQUIRED-SECTIONS:BEGIN -->` and `<!-- REQUIRED-SECTIONS:END -->`.
   Each non-blank line there must have the form: dash, space, backtick, title, backtick. The title
   is the entry. The script holds no copy of the titles.
   If the file is missing, a marker is missing, the list is empty or a line has another form:
   print `sync-agent-roster.cjs: cannot read the required sections from <repo-relative path>: <reason>`
   to stderr and exit 2. This is the only new exit 2.
2. Agent headings. In each `<root>/.skilled/agents/<name>.md`, a numbered section is a line of the
   form `## <digits>. <title>` outside a fenced code block (a line starting with three backticks
   opens or closes a fence). `## 0b. X` is not a numbered section. Keep them in file order.
3. A heading meets a required entry when its title starts with the entry (case-sensitive).
   Use the first heading that meets it.
4. Exceptions. A registry agent record may hold `templateExceptions`: an array of objects
   `{ "section": "<required entry>", "reason": "<text>" }`. A required entry listed there is skipped
   for that agent, for presence and for order.
     an exception whose section is not a required entry  ->  `template exception names no required section: <name>: <section>`
     an exception with a missing or empty reason          ->  `template exception without a reason: <name>: <section>`
5. Problems, one line each, added to the same list the other checks use:
     the file has no numbered section at all              ->  `agent file has no numbered sections: <name>`   (and nothing else for that file)
     a required entry no heading meets                     ->  `missing required section: <name>: <entry>`
     the entries that are present are not in the template's order
                                                           ->  `required section out of order: <name>: <entry>`
       Walk the required entries in order and keep the file position of the last one found. An
       entry found at an earlier position than that is out of order: report it and do not advance.
6. Put the template parsing in one helper and the per-agent comparison in another, each with a
   comment that says why it exists. The check writes nothing.

THE TESTS
The fixture agents are bare today, so they would all fail the new check. Change `beforeEach` so the
temp tree holds a fixture template at the path above with a three-entry list (`CORE WORKFLOW`,
`CAPABILITY SCAN`, `OUTPUT VERIFICATION`), and so every fixture agent file, including the fixture
`orchestrate.md`, carries those three numbered sections in that order. The seven existing tests
must pass with their assertions unchanged.
Add exactly five tests (names are exact):
  it('flags a missing section')                           one agent file loses its `CAPABILITY SCAN` section: exits 1, prints `missing required section:` with the agent name and `CAPABILITY SCAN`
  it('flags a renamed section')                           one agent file's heading becomes `## 2. ROUTING SCAN`: exits 1, prints `missing required section:` with the agent name and `CAPABILITY SCAN`
  it('flags an out-of-order section')                     one agent file has `OUTPUT VERIFICATION` before `CAPABILITY SCAN`: exits 1, prints `required section out of order:`
  it('accepts a section the registry excepts')            one agent file lacks `CAPABILITY SCAN` and its registry record lists it under `templateExceptions` with a reason: exits 0
  it('exits 2 when the required list cannot be parsed')   the fixture template loses its END marker: exits 2 and stderr contains `cannot read the required sections`

